{"category": "manualAnswer", "expect": {"calls": [], "readingAccepted": true}, "group": "fieldAssist", "id": "safety-3-1-answer", "offered": [], "passages": {"p1": "Emergency and shutdown\n- Emergency shutdown: turn off unit power and close the manual and main gas valves (SM p.64). The installer should have labelled these controls.\n- Do not use a furnace that has been under water. A flood-damaged furnace must be inspected and its gas controls, control-system parts, and electrical parts replaced before use (II p.61, SM p.63)."}, "reading": {"answer": "Emergency shutdown: turn off unit power and close the manual and main gas valves (SM p.64).", "found": true, "passage": "p1", "quote": "Emergency shutdown: turn off unit power and close the manual and main gas valves (SM p.64)."}, "source": "safety.md", "turn": "Before I start — emergency and shutdown: anything I need to know?", "vault": "lennox-slp99"}
{"category": "manualAnswer", "expect": {"calls": [], "readingAccepted": true}, "group": "fieldAssist", "id": "safety-3-2-answer", "offered": [], "passages": {"p1": "Emergency and shutdown\n- Emergency shutdown: turn off unit power and close the manual and main gas valves (SM p.64). The installer should have labelled these controls.\n- Do not use a furnace that has been under water. A flood-damaged furnace must be inspected and its gas controls, control-system parts, and electrical parts replaced before use (II p.61, SM p.63)."}, "reading": {"answer": "Do not use a furnace that has been under water.", "found": true, "passage": "p1", "quote": "Do not use a furnace that has been under water."}, "source": "safety.md", "turn": "Before I start — emergency and shutdown: anything I need to know?", "vault": "lennox-slp99"}
{"category": "manualAnswer", "expect": {"calls": [], "readingAccepted": true}, "group": "fieldAssist", "id": "safety-4-1-answer", "offered": [], "passages": {"p1": "Flame rollout and lockouts\n- The two flame rollout switches are manual reset, inside the burner box. If tripped, confirm adequate combustion air and find the cause before resetting; E200 is the hard lockout for an open or previously open rollout circuit (II p.64, SM p.19).\n- Soft lockouts reset automatically after one hour with a call for heat active, or by cycling the call for heat or power. Hard lockout is reset by cycling power to the control (II p.71). Six manually exited soft lockouts in 15 minutes hard-locks the control with E118 (SM p.19)."}, "reading": {"answer": "The two flame rollout switches are manual reset, inside the burner box.", "found": true, "passage": "p1", "quote": "The two flame rollout switches are manual reset, inside the burner box."}, "source": "safety.md", "turn": "Before I start — flame rollout and lockouts: anything I need to know?", "vault": "lennox-slp99"}
{"category": "manualAnswer", "expect": {"calls": [], "readingAccepted": true}, "group": "fieldAssist", "id": "safety-4-2-answer", "offered": [], "passages": {"p1": "Flame rollout and lockouts\n- The two flame rollout switches are manual reset, inside the burner box. If tripped, confirm adequate combustion air and find the cause before resetting; E200 is the hard lockout for an open or previously open rollout circuit (II p.64, SM p.19).\n- Soft lockouts reset automatically after one hour with a call for heat active, or by cycling the call for heat or power. Hard lockout is reset by cycling power to the control (II p.71). Six manually exited soft lockouts in 15 minutes hard-locks the control with E118 (SM p.19)."}, "reading": {"answer": "Soft lockouts reset automatically after one hour with a call for heat active, or by cycling the call for heat or power.", "found": true, "passage": "p1", "quote": "Soft lockouts reset automatically after one hour with a call for heat active, or by cycling the call for heat or power."}, "source": "safety.md", "turn": "Before I start — flame rollout and lockouts: anything I need to know?", "vault": "lennox-slp99"}
{"category": "manualAnswer", "expect": {"calls": [], "readingAccepted": true}, "group": "fieldAssist", "id": "safety-5-1-answer", "offered": [], "passages": {"p1": "R-454B (A2L) refrigerant detection\n- The ignition control is factory enabled for Lennox A2L refrigerant systems. Disabling refrigerant detection on an A2L system is prohibited by safety codes (SM p.1).\n- On a detected leak (E150) the control drops 24 V to the thermostat, runs the blower at high speed to purge, and holds it for the rest of a seven-minute cycle. Do not defeat this; the fault cannot be cleared while the sensor still reports a leak (SM p.22 and p.61).\n- Use only Lennox-approved evaporator coils and LGWP sensors, or the coil manufacturer's recommended sensor with a non-Lennox coil (II p.53)."}, "reading": {"answer": "The ignition control is factory enabled for Lennox A2L refrigerant systems.", "found": true, "passage": "p1", "quote": "The ignition control is factory enabled for Lennox A2L refrigerant systems."}, "source": "safety.md", "turn": "Before I start — r-454B (A2L) refrigerant detection: anything I need to know?", "vault": "lennox-slp99"}
